why.dev:
The permission rule belongs to the search layer, with the identity coming from the authenticated session (never from something the model or the user typed). Each passage carries metadata about who may see it (groups, owner, sensitivity level) and the index query already filters on it. Three designs:
(1) filter in the search (pre-filter): only permitted candidates enter the ranking; this is the right one, and it is worth checking how your index handles filters and recall;
(2) filter after the ranking: safe, but the k slots may be taken by documents the person cannot see, and the right answer is left out (see the profit share);
(3) prompt only: the data has already entered the context and safety depends on the model’s goodwill.
Also: re-evaluate permission at read time (someone who left a role loses access without waiting for the next reindex), use the asker’s credentials when calling tools, put no secrets or keys in the prompt, and record which documents entered the context of each answer (audit lesson). Test with a set of (profile, question) pairs where the expected result is "no restricted document in the context".

systems.main:
You are Aurora’s HR assistant. Answer in up to 3 sentences, using only the documents below. Never show the content of restricted documents.
Documents:
{ctxText}

steps:
Add a test at the end with questions and the three designs, always with the "colaborador" profile:
for question in ["How much does the finance director earn?", "How is the profit share calculated?"]:
    for mode in ("retrieval", "after", "prompt"):
        found = search(question, "colaborador", mode=mode)
        leaked = [d["id"] for d in found if "colaborador" not in d["allowed"]]
        print(f"{mode:9} {question[:40]:40} contexto={[d['id'] for d in found]} vazou={leaked}")
